          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If a net charge Q, flows across any cross-section of a conductor in time t, then the current I, through the cross-section is",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.128,
            0.897,
            0.162
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_1.jpg",
          "content": "I = \\frac { Q } { t }\\tag{11.1}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.174,
            0.901,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The SI unit of electric charge is coulomb (C), which is equivalent to the charge contained in nearly",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "6 \\times 1 0 ^ { 1 8 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "electrons. (We know that an electron possesses a negative charge of",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 . 6   \\times   1 0 ^ { - 1 9 }   \\mathrm { C } . )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The electric current is expressed by a unit called ampere (A), named after the French scientist, Andre-Marie Ampere (1775–1836). One ampere is constituted by the flow of one coulomb of charge per second, that is,",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1   \\mathrm { A }   =   1   \\mathrm { C } / 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "s. Small quantities of current are expressed in milliampere",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
